For every $m\ge2$ there is an integer $s=s(m)$ with the following property. Given $n,r,\chi$, finite lists satisfying <a href="#eq:scale-list-closure" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:scale-list-closure">[eq:scale-list-closure]</a>, and tolerances $0<\tau<1/4$, $\eta>0$, there are admissible parameters and step-at-most-$s$ models such that the following conclusions hold along the fixed ultrafilter $\mathcal U$ in $w$.*


1.  *For each $B,a,c$, $$\label{eq:prediction-calibration}
     \lim_{\mathcal U}\mathbb P\bigl(
     \chi(h_Bat_B)=c,\ S_{B,a,c}(t_B)\le2\tau\bigr)
     \le3\tau+\eta.$$*



2.  *Fix a chain $B_1,\ldots,B_m$, $b\in\mathcal B$ and a color $c\in[r]$. Put $$c_d=h_{B_d}b_{B_d},\qquad
     f_d(y)=\mathbf 1_{\chi(c_dy)=c},\qquad
     \nu_d=\nu_{B_d},\qquad S_d=S_{B_d,b_{B_d},c}.$$ Here $f_d$ is defined on positive integers and may be extended by zero elsewhere. For $\varnothing\ne J\subseteq[m]$ write $d(J)=\max J$ and $$\begin{aligned}
     L_J(z)&=\sum_{k\in J}\frac{c_k}{c_{d(J)}}z_k,
     \label{eq:sum-forms}\\
     U(z)&=\prod_{\varnothing\ne J\subseteq[m]}
     \mathbf 1_{\chi(\prod_{k\in J}c_kz_k)=c}.
     \label{eq:product-mask}
    \end{aligned}$$ Then the nonnegative weighted count $$\label{eq:weighted-count}
     \mathcal I_{b,c,\mathbf B}=
     \mathbb E_{z\sim\bigotimes_{d=1}^m\mu_{i_d}}
     U(z)\prod_{d=1}^m\nu_d(z_d)
     \prod_{\substack{J\subseteq[m]\\|J|\ge2}}
     (f_{d(J)}\nu_{d(J)})(L_J(z))$$ satisfies, for $z(t)_d=t_{B_d}$, $$\label{eq:prediction-counting}
     \lim_{\mathcal U}
     \abs*{\mathcal I_{b,c,\mathbf B}
     -\mathbb E_t U(z(t))
     \prod_{\substack{J\subseteq[m]\\|J|\ge2}}
     S_{d(J)}(L_J(z(t)))}\le\eta.$$

For every $n,r,s$ there are finite lists $\mathcal B,\mathcal A$ satisfying <a href="#eq:scale-list-closure" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:scale-list-closure">[eq:scale-list-closure]</a> and a constant $\delta>0$, depending only on $n,r,s$, such that the following holds. For any admissible family, any system of step-at-most-$s$ models, and any fixed $\tau>0$, define $\mathsf A_w$ to be the event that some $b\in\mathcal B$ satisfies, simultaneously for every block $B$, color $c\in[r]$, and subset $D\subseteq\mathcal E_B$, $$\label{eq:alignment-implication}
 \begin{split}
 S_{B,b_B,c}(t_B)>2\tau\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
 S_{B,b_B,c}\left(
 t_B+\sum_{A\in D}\frac{h_Ab_A}{h_Bb_B}t_A\right)>\tau.
 \end{split}$$ Then $$\label{eq:alignment-probability}
 \liminf_{w\to\infty}\mathbb P(\mathsf A_w)\ge\delta.$$ In particular, $\delta$ is independent of $\tau$ and of the model complexity bound.

Let $F$ and $G$ be fixed nonzero integer polynomials in disjoint tuples of independent prime variables. Each variable is sampled with probability proportional to $1/p$ in a dyadic interval $[Y,2Y)$. Let $L$ be the smallest of these lower endpoints. For all sufficiently large $w,L$, $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathbb P(FG=0)&\ll_{F,G}\frac{\log L}{L},\label{eq:polynomial-zero-bound}\\
 \mathbb P\bigl(FG\ne0,\ \gcd(|F|_{>w},|G|_{>w})>1\bigr)
 &\ll_{F,G}\frac1w+\frac{\log L}{\sqrt L}.
 \label{eq:rough-coprimality-bound}
\end{aligned}$$ The constants are independent of the ratios between the dyadic endpoints. Consequently two independent tuples from any of the constructed pools have coprime rough polynomial values with probability $1-o(1)$. The same assertion holds after restricting each tuple to a prime-only event of probability $1-o(1)$.

Put $q_{\mathrm{mask}}=2^m-1$ and $K_m=q_{\mathrm{mask}}2^{q_{\mathrm{mask}}}$. Starting from <a href="#eq:correlation-initial" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:correlation-initial">[eq:correlation-initial]</a>, one obtains a family $\mathcal R$ of at most $K_m$ pairwise nonparallel row templates, with one distinguished row $*$, and functions $f_R$ that may depend on the introduced primes, such that $$|\mathcal C|^{2^{q_{\mathrm{mask}}}}
 \le C_m\left|\mathbb E_{\boldsymbol p,z}
                 \prod_{R\in\mathcal R}f_R(\ell_R(z))\right|+o(1).
 \label{eq:mask-removal-output}$$ Here $|f_R|\le W_R$, the distinguished function is exactly $f_*=g_*$, its support is $J_*$, and every prime parameter has the law $\lambda$, independently before deletion of exceptional tuples. The row templates, the number of parameters, and $C_m$ depend only on $m$ and the choice of $J_*$. They are independent of the scales and functions.

With the rows, good-tuple law, and modulus of Lemma <a href="#lem:row-directions" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:row-directions">17</a>, fix $J_0\in\mathbb N$ and put $$L(\boldsymbol p)=\left\lfloor\frac{R}{J_0M(\boldsymbol p)}\right\rfloor.
 \label{eq:correlation-shift-length}$$ Independently conditional on the primes, let every $u_R^0,u_R^1$ be uniform on $[0,L(\boldsymbol p))\cap\mathbb Z$, for $R\ne *$. Then $$\left|\mathbb E_{\boldsymbol p,z}\prod_{I\in\mathcal R}f_I(\ell_I(z))
 \right|^{2^d}
 \le C_m\left|\mathbb E_{\boldsymbol p,z,u}
       \prod_{\omega\in\{0,1\}^d}
       g_*\left(\ell_*(z)+M(\boldsymbol p)
                    \sum_{R\ne *}u_R^{\omega_R}\right)\right|+o(1).
 \label{eq:additive-elimination-output}$$ The constants are independent of $J_0$; the error is for fixed $J_0$.

For every fixed $b\geq0$ and dual tests $\mathcal D_1,\ldots,\mathcal D_b$ at the same block, $$\label{eq:prediction-dual-products}
 \mathbb E_{\mu_i}(
u-1)\prod_{q=1}^b\mathcal D_q=o(1)$$ uniformly over their inputs. The tests may use different permissible gaps and different cube types. If $d_*$ bounds the dimensions of these types and $A_*=2^{2^{d_*}-1}$, then for every fixed positive integer $b$, $$\label{eq:prediction-dual-moments}
 \mathbb E_{\mu_i}(1+
u)|\mathcal D|^b\leq2A_*^b+o(1).$$ For any fixed $K>A_*$, replacing every test by its clipping to $[-K,K]$ changes it by $o(1)$ in every fixed $L^p((1+
u)\mu_i)$ norm, and <a href="#eq:prediction-dual-products" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:prediction-dual-products">[eq:prediction-dual-products]</a> remains true for the clipped tests.

For every $\varepsilon>0$, a master count $N$ depending only on $n,r,|\mathcal A|,\varepsilon$ can be chosen so that there are indices $$k_1<j_1<k_2<j_2<\cdots<k_n<j_n$$ and $[0,1]$-valued piecewise models $S_{B,a,c}$ at pivot $i=j_u$ on intervals of length $R_{k_u}$ such that $$\label{eq:prediction-coarse-approximation}
 \|S_{B,a,c}-P_{j_u,k_u}F_{B,a,c}\|_2\leq\varepsilon.$$ For a chain on the principal indices $j_1,\ldots,j_n$, let $l$ be the padding index immediately before its first pivot. At every block $B=T\cup\{j_u\}$ of the chain, $$\label{eq:prediction-fine-projection}
 \|P_{j_u,l}(F_{B,a,c}-S_{B,a,c})\|_2\leq2\varepsilon.$$ The families $S$ have bounded complexity after $N$ and $\varepsilon$ are fixed.

Fix a cube type of dimension $d$, a gap $l<i$, and $J_0\geq1$. Assume $2(2^d)-2\leq s$. For every $\gamma>0$ there is $\kappa=\kappa(d,J_0,\gamma)>0$, independent of the master count, the gap, the cutoffs, and the cell probabilities, with the following property. If $h:\mathbb Z\to[-1,1]$ is a family with $\|P_{i,l}h\|_2<\kappa$, then $$\label{eq:prediction-subgroup-conclusion}
 \lim_{\mathcal U}\left|
 \mathbb E_{\boldsymbol p,y,u}
 \prod_{\omega\subset[d]}
 h\left(y+M_{\boldsymbol p}
       \sum_{j\in\omega}(u_j^1-u_j^0)\right)\right|
 \leq\gamma+O_d(J_0^{-1}).$$ Here $y\sim\mu_i$, and primes and shifts have the laws in <a href="#eq:prediction-dual-test" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:prediction-dual-test">[eq:prediction-dual-test]</a>. The constant in the last term depends only on $d$.

Under the hypotheses of Lemma <a href="#lem:adapted-factorization" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:adapted-factorization">32</a>, retain the subsequence and its data. Define probability measures $$m_{\beta,r}^{[q]}
 =\text{Haar image on }
 h(\beta)^{\mathrm{diag}} C^q(H_\bullet)
 \sigma_r^{\mathrm{diag}}\Lambda^{\{0,1\}^q}/
              \Lambda^{\{0,1\}^q}.
 \label{eq:local-cube-haar}$$ The empirical point laws satisfy $$\frac1{\lfloor L\rfloor}\sum_{0\leq b<\lfloor L\rfloor}
       \delta_{P_L(b)\Lambda}
 \ \Longrightarrow
 \frac1{d_0}\sum_{r=0}^{d_0-1}\int_0^1 m_{\beta,r}^{[0]}\,d\beta.
 \label{eq:joint-point-law}$$ For every fixed cube dimension $q$, every $\beta\in(0,1)$, every residue $r\bmod d_0$, and every bounded Lipschitz test $F$ on $(K/\Lambda)^{\{0,1\}^q}$, $$\lim_{\alpha\downarrow0}\limsup_{L\to\infty}
 \left|
 \mathbb E_{\substack{b_0/L\in[\beta,\beta+\alpha),\ b_0\equiv r\ (d_0)\\
                 b_j/L\in[0,\alpha),\ b_j\equiv0\ (d_0),\ 1\leq j\leq q}}
 F\bigl((P_L(b_0+\sum_{j=1}^q e_jb_j)\Lambda)_e\bigr)
 -\int F\,dm_{\beta,r}^{[q]}
 \right|=0.
 \label{eq:local-cube-limit-order}$$ The inputs in this average are independent and uniform on the specified integer progressions. The same assertion holds for boxes with side lengths between $c\alpha L$ and $C\alpha L$, whose base coordinates are within $C\alpha L$ of $\beta L$ and whose increments have absolute value at most $C\alpha L$, where $c,C>0$ are fixed before either limit. For fixed bounds the assertions are uniform over the tests and these boxes. In particular, arbitrary fixed-modulus progression restrictions are permitted if the base has residue $r$ and all increments have residue zero modulo $d_0$. Every progression modulus is fixed before the length limit, which always precedes the locality limit. The same factorization supplies these conclusions for every fixed $q$.

For given $n,r,s$, there are a positive integer $q_0$ and finite lists of scale updates and word jumps with the following property, uniformly over the actions and palette maps just specified and the initial centers at all pivots. Start the scale tuple at $(1,\ldots,1)$, process the pivots increasingly, and fix an order of their targets. At each pivot one can choose updates <a href="#eq:alignment-scale-update" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:alignment-scale-update">[eq:alignment-scale-update]</a> and jumps from these lists so that, at the resulting scale $b$ and center, the terminal palette comparisons hold simultaneously for all its targets. These comparisons are <a href="#eq:alignment-word-requirement" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:alignment-word-requirement">[eq:alignment-word-requirement]</a> with leading multiplier $d_B$ and word $$\label{eq:alignment-terminal-word}
 \prod_{A\in D}\bigl((B,A),d_A/d_B\bigr)[b],$$ in a fixed order, for every target $B$, every $D\subset\mathcal E_B$, and every multiplier tuple $d$ obtainable from later prescribed updates. When later pivots are processed, the center at this pivot is left fixed: these terminal requirements already include all the later scale changes.*

*All these lists depend only on $n,r,s$. There are finite lists $\mathcal B\subset\mathbb Q_{>0}^n$ and $\mathcal A\subset\mathbb Q_{>0}$ containing, respectively, the final scale tuples and every palette index consulted during the construction. All intermediate scales belong to finite lists as well. The list $\mathcal A$ also contains every required product of entries of a tuple in $\mathcal B$. All powers used in <a href="#eq:alignment-letter" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:alignment-letter">[eq:alignment-letter]</a> are integral. If each $L_e$ also translates an auxiliary integer coordinate $v_e$ by $1$, all auxiliary indices visited from $v=0$ lie in a prescribed finite slot list.
